{"name":"Galaxy Blue","hex":"#2d5284","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"galaxy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The Milky Way; the apparent band of concentrated stars which appears in the night sky over earth. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the collections of many millions or billions of stars, galactic dust, black holes, etc. existing as independent and coherent systems, of which there are billions in the known universe."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with galaxies.","To gather together into a luminous whole."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/galaxy"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Galaxy","blocks":[{"text":"A galaxy is a physical system of stars, planetary systems, stellar remnants, substellar objects, interstellar gas, dust, and dark matter bound together by gravity. The word is derived from the Greek galaxias (γαλαξίας), meaning 'milky', a reference to the Milky Way galaxy that contains the Solar System. Galaxies, averaging an estimated 100 million stars, range in size from dwarfs with less than a thousand stars to the largest galaxies known—supergiants with one hundred trillion stars, each orbiting its galaxy's center of mass. Most of the mass in a typical galaxy is in the form of dark matter, with only a few percent of that mass visible in the form of stars and nebulae. Supermassive black holes are a common feature at the centers of galaxies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg/330px-NGC_4414_%28NASA-med%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galaxy","wikidata":"Q318"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Probing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Galaxy cluster SDSS J1152+3313. SDSS stands for Sloan Digital Sky Survey, J for Julian epoch, and 1152+3313 for right ascension and declination respectively.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProbing_the_distant_past_SDSS_J1152%2B3313.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Herschel-Galaxy.png/500px-Herschel-Galaxy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The shape of the Milky Way as estimated from star counts by William Herschel in 1785; the Solar System was assumed to be near the center.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHerschel-Galaxy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Pic_iroberts1.jpg/500px-Pic_iroberts1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Pic_iroberts1.jpg/500px-Pic_iroberts1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photograph of the \"Great Andromeda Nebula\" by Isaac Roberts, 1899, later identified as the Andromeda Galaxy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APic_iroberts1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/500px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/1280px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Andromeda_galaxy.jpg/500px-Andromeda_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAndromeda_galaxy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Hubble_sequence_photo.png/500px-Hubble_sequence_photo.png?u